PATCH 515: e1a6 posted a stale register into the op-0x54 inbox instead of the object's own position.

asm 0xe1a6-0xe1d0:
    e1a6: 57           push di
    e1a7: 8d 7d 04     lea  di,[di+4]              ; the object's (X,Y) dword pair
    e1ac: 8e 2e 2e ea  mov  gs,[0xea2e] ; e1b0: mov si,[0xea2c]
    e1b4: 66 65 89 9c f2 03  mov gs:[si+0x3f2],ebx ; the op-0x54 inbox
    e1bc: bb 54 00     mov  bx,0x54 ; mov [0xea10],bx ; e1c3: call 0xe339
    e1c6: 5f           pop  di
    e1c7: 88 45 18     mov  [di+0x18],al
    e1ca: 8a 45 0d     mov  al,[di+0xd] ; e1cd: sub al,[di+0x18] ; ret

Ghidra threaded the posted value as a parameter of e1a6, and NONE of its three callers sets BX -- b63e
(FUN_0000_b5e7), b886 and b9fa are all the same shape: update [di+4]/[di+8]/[di+0xc], test
`cmpw [di+0xd],0x80`, call e1a6, branch on the sign of the returned AL.  So in the port the posted value
was an uninitialised stack slot: a stable 0xffffcc38 under gcc -m32 and a different stable value under
emcc.

That is the remaining native<->wasm divergence in two missions, both watchpointed to this exact store
with the whole DGROUP identical on the preceding tick:
    AZER2   t=334   DGROUP:0xc006 == obj(0xbfee)+0x18   native 84 / wasm 67
    SYRIA1  t=3703  DGROUP:0xb20f                       native 43 / wasm 22

The posted value must be THIS object's position pair, on three independent grounds: [di+0x18] is
compared against [di+0xd], the ground height e1d1 stores for the SAME object (via adcd/9db1), and b5e7
has just moved that object; the extender-side decode of the op-0x54 service already records the inbox as
"the DGROUP near offset of a position pair (X,Y dwords), written by e1d1/e1a6/adcd/9db1 as obj+4"; and
`lea di,[di+4]` at 0xe1a7 computes precisely that and is otherwise dead.

CONFIDENCE, stated honestly: the encoding at 0xe1b4 names EBX, not EDI, so this reconstruction rests on
the three arguments above rather than on the register field.

VERIFIED by where the divergence MOVES.  Before this patch AZER2 diverged at t=334 in DGROUP block 24
(0xc000, the object roster) and SYRIA1 at t=3703 in block 17 (0xb200) -- both watchpointed to this store.
After it, both diverge in a DIFFERENT block, 41 (0xe200, the extender interface region), two ticks later.
The carrier this patch addresses is therefore closed and a distinct one is now first; parity itself does
not close yet, and the 47-mission progress sweep moves (INDIA/SAUDI 9/14 -> 6/14) because the terrain
height every object queries is no longer a constant, so genuinely new code runs -- the same pattern as
every other step of this cascade.
The definitive register-level check, if it is ever in doubt again, is the DOSBox write-trace on the inbox
(`FIST_MEMARM_BOOT=1 FISTLOG=<p> FIST_WATCHFLAT=<engine-flat of TCB+0x3f2>`).
